WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.765
Graph diffusion models

00:00:01.576 --> 00:00:02.861
do memorize after

00:00:02.673 --> 00:00:03.168
all

00:00:03.702 --> 00:00:04.657
A new paper

00:00:04.469 --> 00:00:06.143
by Adarsh Jamadandi

00:00:05.954 --> 00:00:07.369
and Nicolas Keriven

00:00:07.216 --> 00:00:08.124
is titled Your

00:00:07.936 --> 00:00:09.433
Discrete Graph Diffusion

00:00:09.244 --> 00:00:10.482
Model is Secretly

00:00:10.294 --> 00:00:11.202
Memorizing

00:00:11.734 --> 00:00:12.866
These models add

00:00:12.677 --> 00:00:13.797
noise to nodes

00:00:13.609 --> 00:00:14.493
and edges

00:00:14.632 --> 00:00:15.929
A denoiser trained

00:00:15.741 --> 00:00:16.554
on a single

00:00:16.366 --> 00:00:17.415
graph seems to

00:00:17.226 --> 00:00:18.617
generate novel graphs

00:00:18.476 --> 00:00:19.679
and metrics like

00:00:19.597 --> 00:00:21.023
edge overlap find

00:00:20.835 --> 00:00:21.790
no sign of

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:22.603
memorization

00:00:23.126 --> 00:00:24.246
By analyzing the

00:00:24.058 --> 00:00:25.861
reverse diffusion trajectories

00:00:26.027 --> 00:00:26.993
the authors show

00:00:26.805 --> 00:00:27.642
for the first

00:00:27.454 --> 00:00:28.491
time that graph

00:00:28.303 --> 00:00:29.647
diffusion models do

00:00:29.459 --> 00:00:30.319
memorize

00:00:30.458 --> 00:00:31.389
The work goes

00:00:31.201 --> 00:00:32.309
to the NeurIPS

00:00:32.121 --> 00:00:33.288
Bento workshop in

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:33.771
Sydney

00:00:34.302 --> 00:00:35.316
Full story on

00:00:35.127 --> 00:00:36.023
the dashboard
